  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLatest satellite imagery shows an MCV located over the central\nportion of our forecast area. Scattered rain showers accompany\nthis MCV and will continue through the this morning before the\nMCV departs to the east by sunrise. Very limited instability\nshould prevent additional heavy rain and subsequent flash flood\nthreat through most of this morning. Therefore, the flood watch\nhas been cancelled a few hours early.\n\nAnother shortwave is working its way across northern Illinois\nand will become the focus for additional convective development\nthis afternoon. Most hi-res models are keying in convection\nbeginning to develop near the I-75 corridor early this \nafternoon, and growing upscale as it moves eastward across the\nrest of the area. Sufficient build-up of moderate instability\nand the presence of deep layer shear around 20-30 knots should\nsupport a marginal severe weather threat. Strong gusty winds\nwill be the primary severe weather hazard.\n\nAn extremely humid airmass characterized by PWAT in the 2-2.25\"\nrange and surface dew points in the low to mid 70s will support\nefficient, heavy rainfall within any thunderstorm that traverses\nthe area. Progressive nature of convection this afternoon should\nprevent a widespread flooding issue, but particularly vulnerable\nareas (such as urban areas, or locations that have received\nexcessive rainfall in the past few days) could pose a localized\nflash flood risk. \n\nIsolated to scattered rain showers will linger Sunday night \ninto Monday morning across Northeast Ohio and Northwest \nPennsylvania.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA cold front will move southeastward across the area on\nWednesday, with a line of showers thunderstorms moving impacting\nthe region. Confidence continues to increase in area/timing,\nwith PoPs increasing to 60-80% areawide, particularly during the\nafternoon hours. Severe weather is a possibility, though its\nquestionable how much instability there will be to work with.",
